{"type": "answer", "run_id": "daily_exhausted-20261110T183141Z", "scenario": "daily_exhausted", "source": "stand-in", "setting": "large", "setting_def": {"piece_chars": 2400, "k": 3, "rerank": false, "router": true}, "drafter": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "qid": "Q12", "request_id": "daily_exhausted-20261110T183141Z-0001", "start_time": "2026-11-10T18:32:07.895+00:00", "arrival_offset_s": 26.478, "patience_s": 22.19, "gave_up": false, "waited_ms": 4.9, "degraded": true, "reply": {"request_id": "daily_exhausted-20261110T183141Z-0001", "question": "What is Moshi's word error rate on noisy Indian English telephone calls?", "kind": "passages", "level": 3, "text": "No written answer this time: here are the most relevant passages from the papers, quoted as they are. A written answer should be possible in about 603 s.\n[S1] 2410.00037v1 pp.17-18 §4.2: \"We use a first audio collection of 7 million hours, which we call the unsupervised audio dataset, of readily available audio content, the majority of which contains English speech. We transcribe the training set with Whisper (Radford et al., 2023), using the large-v3 model. We use this data for the audio pre-training phase, during which we do not use the multi-stream approach described in Section 3.4.3, but instead use a single stream of audio representing all speakers at once. Similarly, the text stream described in Section 3.4.4 represents the words coming from all speakers.\"\n[S2] 2410.00037v1 pp.16-17 §3.4.4: \"Note that if t_i = 1, we instead insert EPAD at index 1, and shift the text tokens. We do not insert an EPAD token if it would overwrite a text token from a previous word. As text tokens are more compact than the corresponding audio tokens, there is usually no overlap between words in W_t. In English conversational speech, we observe that padding tokens represent about 65% of the tokens. Deriving streaming ASR and TTS. One can further introduce some delay between the text sequence (W_t), and the audio tokens (A_t,q).\"", "sources": [{"sid": "S1", "cite": "2410.00037v1 pp.17-18 §4.2", "pid": "moshi:2400:0027", "paper": "moshi", "block_ids": ["moshi-p17-b007", "moshi-p17-b008", "moshi-p17-b009", "moshi-p18-b001", "moshi-p18-b002", "moshi-p18-b003"]}, {"sid": "S2", "cite": "2410.00037v1 pp.16-17 §3.4.4", "pid": "moshi:2400:0024", "paper": "moshi", "block_ids": ["moshi-p16-b006", "moshi-p16-b007", "moshi-p16-b008", "moshi-p16-b009", "moshi-p16-b010", "moshi-p17-b001"]}], "notice": "passages only, no model call: no drafter free within 8 s", "eta_s": 603.1, "model_calls": [], "spans": [{"name": "plan", "start_ms": 0.0, "end_ms": 0.9, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {"predicted_wait_s": {"full": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 843.7726907999968, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 855.0046546000012, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 860.1890061000013}, "lean": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 603.1486908000006, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 610.4926545999988, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 607.9010061000008}}, "drafter": null}}, {"name": "retrieve", "start_ms": 0.9, "end_ms": 1.4, "model": null, "outcome": "1 search", "detail": {"query": "What is Moshi's word error rate on noisy Indian English telephone calls?", "papers": null, "k": 2, "evidence": {"best": 0.478, "union": 0.529, "top_bm25": 9.675, "query_terms": 10}}}, {"name": "send", "start_ms": 4.5, "end_ms": 4.5, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {}}], "checks": {}, "released_ms": 4.5, "budget_wait_ms": 0, "tokens_in": 0, "tokens_out": 0, "level_name": "passages", "pipeline_model_calls": []}}
{"type": "answer", "run_id": "daily_exhausted-20261110T183141Z", "scenario": "daily_exhausted", "source": "stand-in", "setting": "large", "setting_def": {"piece_chars": 2400, "k": 3, "rerank": false, "router": true}, "drafter": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "qid": "Q4", "request_id": "daily_exhausted-20261110T183141Z-0002", "start_time": "2026-11-10T18:32:33.990+00:00", "arrival_offset_s": 52.58, "patience_s": 24.01, "gave_up": false, "waited_ms": 4.4, "degraded": true, "reply": {"request_id": "daily_exhausted-20261110T183141Z-0002", "question": "Per Table 1, how does self-attention compare with recurrent layers on complexity per layer, sequential operations and maximum path length?", "kind": "passages", "level": 3, "text": "No written answer this time: here are the most relevant passages from the papers, quoted as they are. A written answer should be possible in about 465 s.\n[S1] 1706.03762v7 pp.6-7 §4: \"The shorter these paths between any combination of positions in the input and output sequences, the easier it is to learn long-range dependencies [12]. Hence we also compare the maximum path length between any two input and output positions in networks composed of the different layer types. As noted in Table 1, a self-attention layer connects all positions with a constant number of sequentially executed operations, whereas a recurrent layer requires O(n) sequential operations.\"\n[S2] 1706.03762v7 pp.5-6 §3.4: \"In the embedding layers, we multiply those weights by √ d_model. Table 1: Maximum path lengths, per-layer complexity and minimum number of sequential operations for different layer types. n is the sequence length, d is the representation dimension, k is the kernel size of convolutions and r the size of the neighborhood in restricted self-attention. Layer Type Complexity per Layer Sequential Maximum Path Length Operations Self-Attention O(n^2 · d) O(1) O(1) Recurrent O(n · d^2) O(n) O(n) Convolutional O(k · n · d^2) O(1) O(log_k(n)) Self-Attention (restricted) O(r · n · d) O(1) O(n/r)\"", "sources": [{"sid": "S1", "cite": "1706.03762v7 pp.6-7 §4", "pid": "attention:2400:0012", "paper": "attention", "block_ids": ["attention-p06-b011", "attention-p06-b012", "attention-p06-b013", "attention-p06-b014", "attention-p07-b001"]}, {"sid": "S2", "cite": "1706.03762v7 pp.5-6 §3.4", "pid": "attention:2400:0010", "paper": "attention", "block_ids": ["attention-p05-b017", "attention-p05-b018", "attention-p06-b001", "attention-p06-b002"]}], "notice": "passages only, no model call: no drafter free within 8 s", "eta_s": 464.7, "model_calls": [], "spans": [{"name": "plan", "start_ms": 0.0, "end_ms": 1.4, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {"predicted_wait_s": {"full": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 575.7596907999978, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 583.1036545999959, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 580.512006099998}, "lean": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 465.16769080000086, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 470.7836545999962, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 464.73600609999994}}, "drafter": null}}, {"name": "retrieve", "start_ms": 1.4, "end_ms": 2.1, "model": null, "outcome": "1 search", "detail": {"query": "Per Table 1, how does self-attention compare with recurrent layers on complexity per layer, sequential operations and maximum path length?", "papers": null, "k": 2, "evidence": {"best": 1.0, "union": 1.0, "top_bm25": 47.014, "query_terms": 13}}}, {"name": "send", "start_ms": 3.9, "end_ms": 3.9, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {}}], "checks": {}, "released_ms": 3.9, "budget_wait_ms": 0, "tokens_in": 0, "tokens_out": 0, "level_name": "passages", "pipeline_model_calls": []}}
{"type": "answer", "run_id": "daily_exhausted-20261110T183141Z", "scenario": "daily_exhausted", "source": "stand-in", "setting": "large", "setting_def": {"piece_chars": 2400, "k": 3, "rerank": false, "router": true}, "drafter": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "qid": "Q2", "request_id": "daily_exhausted-20261110T183141Z-0003", "start_time": "2026-11-10T18:32:49.391+00:00", "arrival_offset_s": 67.979, "patience_s": 38.93, "gave_up": false, "waited_ms": 2.4, "degraded": true, "reply": {"request_id": "daily_exhausted-20261110T183141Z-0003", "question": "Why use several attention heads instead of one, and what configuration did the paper use?", "kind": "passages", "level": 3, "text": "No written answer this time: here are the most relevant passages from the papers, quoted as they are. A written answer should be possible in about 327 s.\n[S1] 1706.03762v7 pp.3-4 §3.2: \"An attention function can be described as mapping a query and a set of key-value pairs to an output, where the query, keys, values, and output are all vectors. The output is computed as a weighted sum Scaled Dot-Product Attention Multi-Head Attention Figure 2: (left) Scaled Dot-Product Attention. (right) Multi-Head Attention consists of several attention layers running in parallel. of the values, where the weight assigned to each value is computed by a compatibility function of the query with the corresponding key.\"\n[S2] 1706.03762v7 pp.4-5 §3.2.2: \"Instead of performing a single attention function with d_model-dimensional keys, values and queries, we found it beneficial to linearly project the queries, keys and values h times with different, learned linear projections to d_k, d_k and d_v dimensions, respectively. On each of these projected versions of queries, keys and values we then perform the attention function in parallel, yielding d_v-dimensional ^4To illustrate why the dot products get large, assume that the components of q and k are independent random variables with mean 0 and variance 1.\"", "sources": [{"sid": "S1", "cite": "1706.03762v7 pp.3-4 §3.2", "pid": "attention:2400:0005", "paper": "attention", "block_ids": ["attention-p03-b007", "attention-p04-b001", "attention-p04-b002", "attention-p04-b003"]}, {"sid": "S2", "cite": "1706.03762v7 pp.4-5 §3.2.2", "pid": "attention:2400:0007", "paper": "attention", "block_ids": ["attention-p04-b015", "attention-p04-b016", "attention-p04-b017", "attention-p05-b001", "attention-p05-b002", "attention-p05-b003", "attention-p05-b004", "attention-p05-b005", "attention-p05-b006"]}], "notice": "passages only, no model call: no drafter free within 8 s", "eta_s": 326.6, "model_calls": [], "spans": [{"name": "plan", "start_ms": 0.0, "end_ms": 0.7, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {"predicted_wait_s": {"full": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 430.3206907999993, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 435.50465460000123, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 429.025006099997}, "lean": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 332.2566908000008, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 336.14465460000065, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 326.6410060999988}}, "drafter": null}}, {"name": "retrieve", "start_ms": 0.8, "end_ms": 1.2, "model": null, "outcome": "1 search", "detail": {"query": "Why use several attention heads instead of one, and what configuration did the paper use?", "papers": null, "k": 2, "evidence": {"best": 0.501, "union": 0.653, "top_bm25": 10.365, "query_terms": 6}}}, {"name": "send", "start_ms": 2.3, "end_ms": 2.3, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {}}], "checks": {}, "released_ms": 2.3, "budget_wait_ms": 0, "tokens_in": 0, "tokens_out": 0, "level_name": "passages", "pipeline_model_calls": []}}
{"type": "answer", "run_id": "daily_exhausted-20261110T183141Z", "scenario": "daily_exhausted", "source": "stand-in", "setting": "large", "setting_def": {"piece_chars": 2400, "k": 3, "rerank": false, "router": true}, "drafter": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "qid": "Q7", "request_id": "daily_exhausted-20261110T183141Z-0004", "start_time": "2026-11-10T18:33:22.233+00:00", "arrival_offset_s": 100.818, "patience_s": 33.02, "gave_up": false, "waited_ms": 3.7, "degraded": true, "reply": {"request_id": "daily_exhausted-20261110T183141Z-0004", "question": "Why do the authors say traditional spoken dialogue systems struggle to feel natural?", "kind": "passages", "level": 3, "text": "No written answer this time: here are the most relevant passages from the papers, quoted as they are. A written answer should be possible in about 499 s.\n[S1] 2410.00037v1 p.2 §1: \"A solution to this challenge is handling the NLU and NLG with an LLM, while the ASR and TTS provide the voice interface during the user’s and the system’s turn respectively (Llama, 2024). This framework supports the current generation of spoken dialogue systems such as Gemini (Gemini et al., 2023) or ChatGPT.^4 Yet, the experience offered by these interfaces remains far from natural conversations. First, latency compounds along the many components of these pipelines, resulting in a typical global latency of several seconds.\"\n[S2] 2410.00037v1 p.1 Abstract: \"We introduce Moshi, a speech-text foundation model and full-duplex spoken dialogue framework. Current systems for spoken dialogue rely on pipelines of independent components, namely voice activity detection, speech recognition, textual dialogue and text-to-speech. Such frameworks cannot emulate the experience of real conversations. First, their complexity induces a latency of several seconds between interactions. Second, text being the intermediate modality for dialogue, non-linguistic information that modifies meaning— such as emotion or non-speech sounds— is lost in the interaction.\"", "sources": [{"sid": "S1", "cite": "2410.00037v1 p.2 §1", "pid": "moshi:2400:0001", "paper": "moshi", "block_ids": ["moshi-p02-b002", "moshi-p02-b003"]}, {"sid": "S2", "cite": "2410.00037v1 p.1 Abstract", "pid": "moshi:2400:0000", "paper": "moshi", "block_ids": ["moshi-p01-b005"]}], "notice": "passages only, no model call: no drafter free within 8 s", "eta_s": 499.0, "model_calls": [], "spans": [{"name": "plan", "start_ms": 0.0, "end_ms": 0.7, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {"predicted_wait_s": {"full": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 558.1816907999964, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 565.9576546000026, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 564.6620060999994}, "lean": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 498.9976907999953, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 505.90965460000007, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 502.4540061000007}}, "drafter": null}}, {"name": "retrieve", "start_ms": 0.7, "end_ms": 1.1, "model": null, "outcome": "1 search", "detail": {"query": "Why do the authors say traditional spoken dialogue systems struggle to feel natural?", "papers": null, "k": 2, "evidence": {"best": 0.426, "union": 0.426, "top_bm25": 14.626, "query_terms": 7}}}, {"name": "send", "start_ms": 3.6, "end_ms": 3.6, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {}}], "checks": {}, "released_ms": 3.6, "budget_wait_ms": 0, "tokens_in": 0, "tokens_out": 0, "level_name": "passages", "pipeline_model_calls": []}}
{"type": "answer", "run_id": "daily_exhausted-20261110T183141Z", "scenario": "daily_exhausted", "source": "stand-in", "setting": "large", "setting_def": {"piece_chars": 2400, "k": 3, "rerank": false, "router": true}, "drafter": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "qid": "Q10", "request_id": "daily_exhausted-20261110T183141Z-0005", "start_time": "2026-11-10T18:33:39.820+00:00", "arrival_offset_s": 118.411, "patience_s": 27.86, "gave_up": false, "waited_ms": 3.9, "degraded": true, "reply": {"request_id": "daily_exhausted-20261110T183141Z-0005", "question": "What is Inner Monologue, and how does it give streaming ASR and TTS?", "kind": "passages", "level": 3, "text": "No written answer this time: here are the most relevant passages from the papers, quoted as they are. A written answer should be possible in about 565 s.\n[S1] 2410.00037v1 p.31 §5.7: \"Metrics. Section 3.4.4 and Appendix C describe how Inner Monologue can provide a streaming TTS or streaming ASR system by simply changing the delay it uses between text and audio tokens. In particular, we train a streaming TTS model by delaying audio tokens by 2 seconds, giving some lookahead to the text tokens, and teacher forcing text tokens at inference. Similarly, we train a streaming ASR model by delaying text tokens by 2 seconds, allowing the model to listen to audio content before generating text tokens. In that case, at inference we teacher force the audio tokens.\"\n[S2] 2410.00037v1 pp.41-42 §7: \"To summarize, we introduce a complete suite of models and recipes, from text LLMs to neural audio codecs and generative audio models, which we combine into a real-time spoken dialogue system with a 160ms theoretical latency able to follow complex multi-turn conversations across 5 minutes. We release both Mimi and Moshi to foster the development of such applications. Additionally, we show how our Inner Monologue method allows designing streaming TTS and streaming ASR just by changing the delay between text and audio tokens.\"", "sources": [{"sid": "S1", "cite": "2410.00037v1 p.31 §5.7", "pid": "moshi:2400:0050", "paper": "moshi", "block_ids": ["moshi-p31-b003", "moshi-p31-b004", "moshi-p31-b005"]}, {"sid": "S2", "cite": "2410.00037v1 pp.41-42 §7", "pid": "moshi:2400:0065", "paper": "moshi", "block_ids": ["moshi-p41-b005", "moshi-p41-b006", "moshi-p41-b007", "moshi-p41-b008", "moshi-p42-b001"]}], "notice": "passages only, no model call: no drafter free within 8 s", "eta_s": 564.8, "model_calls": [], "spans": [{"name": "plan", "start_ms": 0.0, "end_ms": 0.7, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {"predicted_wait_s": {"full": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 784.6826908000003, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 796.3466545999981, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 801.9630060999989}, "lean": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 564.7946908000013, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 573.0026546000008, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 572.1390060999984}}, "drafter": null}}, {"name": "retrieve", "start_ms": 0.7, "end_ms": 1.1, "model": null, "outcome": "1 search", "detail": {"query": "What is Inner Monologue, and how does it give streaming ASR and TTS?", "papers": null, "k": 2, "evidence": {"best": 1.0, "union": 1.0, "top_bm25": 16.835, "query_terms": 6}}}, {"name": "send", "start_ms": 3.8, "end_ms": 3.8, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {}}], "checks": {}, "released_ms": 3.8, "budget_wait_ms": 0, "tokens_in": 0, "tokens_out": 0, "level_name": "passages", "pipeline_model_calls": []}}
{"type": "answer", "run_id": "daily_exhausted-20261110T183141Z", "scenario": "daily_exhausted", "source": "stand-in", "setting": "large", "setting_def": {"piece_chars": 2400, "k": 3, "rerank": false, "router": true}, "drafter": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "qid": "Q11", "request_id": "daily_exhausted-20261110T183141Z-0006", "start_time": "2026-11-10T18:33:48.745+00:00", "arrival_offset_s": 127.326, "patience_s": 60.87, "gave_up": false, "waited_ms": 3.7, "degraded": true, "reply": {"request_id": "daily_exhausted-20261110T183141Z-0006", "question": "What theoretical and practical latency does the paper report?", "kind": "passages", "level": 3, "text": "No written answer this time: here are the most relevant passages from the papers, quoted as they are. A written answer should be possible in about 492 s.\n[S1] 2410.00037v1 pp.25-26 §5.3: \"Table 5 reports results for ablations on the use of an RQ- Transformer. We first replicate the setting of Copet et al. (2023) with the delay pattern of [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7], which means that each level of R VQ token is generated one timestep after the preceding level. In this context, we see that using an RQ-Transformer is not necessary, as it only provides a marginal improvement in perplexity. However, this delay pattern induces a theoretical latency of 8 timesteps, which amounts to 640ms, a latency that is incompatible with the requirements of a real-time dialogue model.\"\n[S2] 2410.00037v1 p.26 §5.3: \"Results - Additional ablations. Table 6 reports additional ablations on additional delay patterns, the weight of the semantic token loss and our proposed Inner Monologue procedure, all using the RQ-Transformer. First, we compare three configurations of delays that are compatible with real-time dialogue. The [0 , 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0] pattern represents the minimal latency of 80ms that can be obtained with Mimi tokens at 12.5Hz.\"", "sources": [{"sid": "S1", "cite": "2410.00037v1 pp.25-26 §5.3", "pid": "moshi:2400:0040", "paper": "moshi", "block_ids": ["moshi-p25-b006", "moshi-p25-b007", "moshi-p26-b001", "moshi-p26-b002"]}, {"sid": "S2", "cite": "2410.00037v1 p.26 §5.3", "pid": "moshi:2400:0041", "paper": "moshi", "block_ids": ["moshi-p26-b003", "moshi-p26-b004"]}], "notice": "passages only, no model call: no drafter free within 8 s", "eta_s": 491.5, "model_calls": [], "spans": [{"name": "plan", "start_ms": 0.0, "end_ms": 0.6, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {"predicted_wait_s": {"full": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 674.2416908000014, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 684.1776545999965, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 687.2020061000003}, "lean": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 491.50569079999696, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 498.4176546000017, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 495.82600609999645}}, "drafter": null}}, {"name": "retrieve", "start_ms": 0.7, "end_ms": 0.9, "model": null, "outcome": "1 search", "detail": {"query": "What theoretical and practical latency does the paper report?", "papers": null, "k": 2, "evidence": {"best": 1.0, "union": 1.0, "top_bm25": 13.445, "query_terms": 4}}}, {"name": "send", "start_ms": 3.6, "end_ms": 3.6, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {}}], "checks": {}, "released_ms": 3.6, "budget_wait_ms": 0, "tokens_in": 0, "tokens_out": 0, "level_name": "passages", "pipeline_model_calls": []}}
{"type": "answer", "run_id": "daily_exhausted-20261110T183141Z", "scenario": "daily_exhausted", "source": "stand-in", "setting": "large", "setting_def": {"piece_chars": 2400, "k": 3, "rerank": false, "router": true}, "drafter": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "qid": "Q3", "request_id": "daily_exhausted-20261110T183141Z-0007", "start_time": "2026-11-10T18:33:58.333+00:00", "arrival_offset_s": 136.921, "patience_s": 7.46, "gave_up": false, "waited_ms": 2.9, "degraded": true, "reply": {"request_id": "daily_exhausted-20261110T183141Z-0007", "question": "How are the positional encodings built, and why sinusoids rather than learned embeddings?", "kind": "passages", "level": 3, "text": "No written answer this time: here are the most relevant passages from the papers, quoted as they are. A written answer should be possible in about 414 s.\n[S1] 1706.03762v7 p.6 §3.5: \"To this end, we add \"positional encodings\" to the input embeddings at the bottoms of the encoder and decoder stacks. The positional encodings have the same dimension d_model as the embeddings, so that the two can be summed. There are many choices of positional encodings, learned and fixed [9]. In this work, we use sine and cosine functions of different frequencies: P E_(pos,2i) = sin(pos/10000^2i/d_model) P E_(pos,2i+1) = cos(pos/10000^2i/d_model) where pos is the position and i is the dimension. That is, each dimension of the positional encoding corresponds to a sinusoid.\"\n[S2] 1706.03762v7 pp.8-9 §6.2: \"In Table 3 rows (B), we observe that reducing the attention key size d_k hurts model quality. This suggests that determining compatibility is not easy and that a more sophisticated compatibility function than dot product may be beneficial. We further observe in rows (C) and (D) that, as expected, bigger models are better, and dropout is very helpful in avoiding over-fitting. In row (E) we replace our sinusoidal positional encoding with learned positional embeddings [9], and observe nearly identical results to the base model.\"", "sources": [{"sid": "S1", "cite": "1706.03762v7 p.6 §3.5", "pid": "attention:2400:0011", "paper": "attention", "block_ids": ["attention-p06-b004", "attention-p06-b005", "attention-p06-b006", "attention-p06-b007", "attention-p06-b008", "attention-p06-b009"]}, {"sid": "S2", "cite": "1706.03762v7 pp.8-9 §6.2", "pid": "attention:2400:0020", "paper": "attention", "block_ids": ["attention-p08-b012", "attention-p08-b013", "attention-p09-b001", "attention-p09-b002", "attention-p09-b003", "attention-p09-b004", "attention-p09-b005"]}], "notice": "passages only, no model call: no drafter free within 8 s", "eta_s": 414.5, "model_calls": [], "spans": [{"name": "plan", "start_ms": 0.0, "end_ms": 0.7, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {"predicted_wait_s": {"full": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 542.3916908000028, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 550.5996546000024, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 549.7360060999999}, "lean": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 414.5196907999998, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 420.56765460000315, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 415.8160061000017}}, "drafter": null}}, {"name": "retrieve", "start_ms": 0.7, "end_ms": 1.0, "model": null, "outcome": "1 search", "detail": {"query": "How are the positional encodings built, and why sinusoids rather than learned embeddings?", "papers": null, "k": 2, "evidence": {"best": 0.719, "union": 0.719, "top_bm25": 24.008, "query_terms": 7}}}, {"name": "send", "start_ms": 2.8, "end_ms": 2.8, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {}}], "checks": {}, "released_ms": 2.8, "budget_wait_ms": 0, "tokens_in": 0, "tokens_out": 0, "level_name": "passages", "pipeline_model_calls": []}}
